 original value.\n2. **Given** a cached value that is a mutable structure, **When** the caller mutates the object returned by get(key) or the original object passed to put, **Then** subsequent get(key) returns the unmutated stored value (detached deep copy on output, copy on input).\n3. **Given** a cache containing key k, **When** get(missing) is called for an absent key, **Then** KeyError is raised and cache state is unchanged.\n4. **Given** a non-empty cache, **When** put is called with tags as a bare string (e.g. \"ab\") or with a non-string entry in the tag iterable, **Then** ValueError is raised and the previous entry for that key (value, tags, recency) plus all other state is unchanged.\n5. **Given** put(key, value, tags=(\"a\", \"a\", \"b\")), **When** the entry is later inspected via tag-based operations, **Then** duplicate tags are deduplicated (the entry carries tags {\"a\", \"b\"}).\n6. **Given** an unhashable key (e.g. a list), **When** put or get is attempted, **Then** the standard unhashable-key error (TypeError) is raised and state is unchanged.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Invalidate by Tag and Manage Membership Lifecycle (Priority: P2)\n\nA library user removes all currently cached entries carrying a given tag in one call and learns how many were removed. Replacing an entry under an existing key drops its old tag memberships. Entries evicted by capacity limits leave no stale tag memberships that could affect later reuse of the same key. len(cache) always counts live entries.\n\n**Why this priority**: Tag-based invalidation is the distinguishing capability over a plain LRU cache; it depends on P1 storage but is independently testable once entries exist.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fully testable by populating entries with overlapping tags, calling invalidate(tag), and asserting exact removal counts, surviving entries, and len(cache); plus a replace-then-invalidate and an evict-then-reuse sequence.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries k1 (tags a,b), k2 (tags a), k3 (no tags), **When** invalidate(\"a\") is called, **Then** exactly k1 and k2 are removed, the return value is 2, k3 remains, and len(cache) reflects only live entries.\n2. **Given** key k with tags (\"a\"), **When** put(k, new_value, tags=(\"b\")) is called, **Then** the old tag membership \"a\" is removed; invalidate(\"a\") returns 0 and invalidate(\"b\") removes k.\n3. **Given** a cache at capacity, **When** new puts evict the least-recently-used entry carrying tag t, **Then** invalidate(t) no longer counts the evicted entry, and a later put of the same key with different tags is not affected by stale memberships of the evicted entry.\n4. **Given** a cache with no entries carrying tag t, **When** invalidate(t) is called, **Then** it returns 0 and state is unchanged.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Bounded Capacity with Strict Construction and Replacement Validation (Priority: P3)\n\nA library user bounds the cache with a positive integer maxsize; every value costs exactly one capacity slot regardless of object size. Invalid maxsize values (including booleans, zero, negatives, non-integers) are rejected at construction. An invalid replacement (bad tags on an existing key) leaves the old value, tags, and recency completely unchanged.\n\n**Why this priority**: Bounding and validation protect memory safety and contract integrity; they are verifiable once P1/P2 exist.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fully testable by constructing with invalid maxsize values (expect rejection), filling a small cache to capacity, observing eviction of the least-recently-used entry, and attempting invalid replacements on live entries.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** maxsize values 0, -1, True, False, 2.5, \"3\", **When** the cache is constructed, **Then** each is rejected (ValueError or TypeError) and no cache is created; positive integers (e.g. 1, 3) are accepted.\n2. **Given** a cache with maxsize 2 holding k1 (oldest) and k2, **When** put(k3) is called, **Then** k1 is evicted, len(cache) is 2, and get(k1) raises KeyError.\n"}